\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is every countable discrete group sofic? For each finite collection of elements and each positive error tolerance, one requires permutations of a finite set that almost preserve the specified products in normalized Hamming distance and almost separate every pair of distinct specified elements.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://web.ma.utexas.edu/users/juschenko/files/soficgroups.pdf}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is every countable discrete group sofic? For each finite collection of elements and each positive error tolerance, one requires permutations of a finite set that almost preserve the specified products in normalized Hamming distance and almost separate every pair of distinct specified elements.

